course: dictionary
lesson: 5355
title: Casual words: kinda
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Kinda is a quick, relaxed way of saying «kind of» in speech. It means a bit, somewhat or sort of. «I'm kinda tired» means you are a bit tired, not completely worn out.
explain: The words around kinda show you its meaning. In «Yeah, kinda,» says Minnie, she agrees a bit, sort of, to her friend's comment that she has two families. In «What kinda game is it?» kinda means a type of.
explain: You will hear kinda in chat and everyday talk, especially in North America. In stories and comics it makes characters sound informal and relaxed, like real friends talking. Marquis «kinda likes the order» means he likes it a bit.
explain: Kinda is slang, so keep it out of schoolwork. Write «kind of», «rather» or «quite» instead.
fact: Kinda is an informal spelling of «kind of».
fact: «Kinda» means a bit, somewhat or sort of.
fact: «Kinda» can also mean a type of, as in «What kinda game is it?»
fact: Slang and informal words belong in chat, speech and comics, not in school writing.
fact: In schoolwork, write «kind of», «rather» or «quite» instead of «kinda».
exercise: «Yeah, kinda,» says Minnie. What does kinda mean here? | Never, not at all | Completely finished | *A bit, sort of | Very loudly
exercise: In «What kinda game is it?», what does kinda mean? | *A type of | A bit | Quickly | Not at all
exercise: You want to write «I'm kinda tired» in a school essay. Which is the best choice? | kinda-tired | *kind of tired | kinda tired | tired, kinda
test: Marquis's friend says his dad is wild. Marquis says he kinda likes the order. What does kinda likes mean? | *Likes it a bit | Hates the noise | Doesn't like it at all | Likes it more than anything in the world
test: In a school story, which word could replace kinda in «I was kinda sad»? | yeah | wow | *quite | totally
exam: Why do writers use kinda in stories and comics? | Because it is the only correct spelling | To show that a character is a robot | To make the story longer and harder to read | *To make characters sound casual and informal
